WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:05.960
John Carpenter and Debra Hill's Halloween
celebrates its 40th anniversary in 2018, and

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.800
the occasion's being marked with a new movie.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.460
Hearkening back to the original film, the
movie will ignore all other sequels in the

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:12.960
series.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:18.220
There have been ten Halloweens since 1978,
including all of the sequels, remakes, and

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:18.800
detours.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.640
Are they really all worth ignoring?

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.980
To answer that question, we're strolling methodically
through Halloween history like Michael Myers,

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.240
starting with the outings we're most ready
to forget.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.780
Halloween 4: The Return of Michael Myers

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:35.460
The greatest sin a movie can commit is being
boring, and The Return of Michael Myers is

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.160
just that.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.260
Picking up ten years after the original, Return
finds Michael waking up from a decade-long

00:00:41.269 --> 00:00:43.829
coma when he overhears he has a niece.

00:00:43.829 --> 00:00:45.980
It doesn't get much more thrilling from there.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.220
We learn that Laurie Strode has died in a
car accident, leaving a young daughter, Jamie.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.380
With nothing better to do, Michael meanders
into town to find her.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:59.840
The Return of Michael Myers promptly turns
into a tepid retread as Michael begins robotically

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:05.590
mowing down teenagers, with flat cinematography
giving the uninspired action nowhere to hide.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.300
It's a movie that coasts on its own concept,
ultimately going nowhere.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:11.700
Halloween (2007)

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.490
Though Rob Zombie was a bold choice to spearhead
this reboot, the result is an overcomplicated,

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:18.840
unpleasant mess.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:24.460
Mr. Zombie has no small amount of style, and
he assembles a solid cast for his reimagining.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.270
Unfortunately, the relentless dreariness and
unstable story structure make it hard to connect

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:29.760
with.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:34.660
Half lengthy backstory, half a remake of Halloween
1, this interesting effort just doesn't add

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:35.160
up.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.900
Halloween: The Curse of Michael Myers

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:43.400
Halloween 6 finds Jamie escaping from a cult
six years after she and her unkillable uncle

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.120
were abducted.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:49.439
She's finally killed by Michael, but not before
giving birth to a baby who winds up in the

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.860
care of Paul Rudd, appearing in one of his
first film roles.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:58.350
The ridiculous plot revolves around the Cult
of Thorn, a Druidic order bent on gaining

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.670
power through ritual sacrifice.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.499
Their leader is revealed to be Dr. Terence
Wynn - a character who appeared in one scene

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.960
of the first Halloween that you certainly
won't remember.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.620
The tangled drama leaves little room for either
scares or fun.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.540
The most compelling reason to watch is to
marvel at Paul Rudd, who seemingly doesn't

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.080
age.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.240
Halloween: Resurrection

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.790
The eighth Halloween begins as a reality TV
satire, with contestants being sent into the

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.400
old Myers house for a night.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.150
The house is full of spooky traps, but the
danger gets real when Michael unexpectedly

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:31.980
comes home.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.060
The first thing he kills is the interesting
concept.

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:40.209
Watching Busta Rhymes yell at a computer is
extremely fun, but even he can't save Resurrection

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.840
from being a dreary slog.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.740
As an added insult, the film opens with a
convoluted retcon to undo the ending of Halloween

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.280
H2O, which was originally intended as a series
finale.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:56.520
As part of this, Jamie Lee Curtis appears
in a sad cameo, being brought back as a resident

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.460
of a mental hospital only to die - again.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.160
Halloween II (2009)

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.140
Mr. Zombie returns to write and direct a sequel
to his remake, and it's a marked improvement

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:08.040
over the first attempt.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:14.799
Zombie chose to shoot Halloween II on 16 millimeter
film, lending it an authentically gritty grindhouse

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.640
feel that sets it apart from more glossy horror
movies.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.220
It helps make the movie a surreal, hallucinatory
feast - a unique turn for the series, even

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.320
if the resulting movie is still not great.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.860
Halloween 5: The Revenge of Michael Myers

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.390
What the fifth Halloween may lack in originality,
it makes up for with fun.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:36.860
Since her last encounter with the Shape, Jamie
has been experiencing hallucinations that

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:39.560
suggest some kind of telepathic link with
her uncle Michael.

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:45.180
The movie is full of 80s-era indulgence, but
the real attraction is Donald Pleasence as

00:03:45.189 --> 00:03:50.019
Dr. Loomis, the long-suffering nemesis of
Michael Myers, going more over-the-top than

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:53.220
ever in his single-minded efforts to stop
the killer for good.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.480
He fails.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.020
Halloween II (1981)

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:02.810
Halloween II continues the story of Halloween
as directly as possible, working desperately

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.440
all the while to feel different.

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:09.560
The sequel also explains Michael's relationship
to Laurie, who's revealed to be his long-lost

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:10.120
sister.

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:15.140
The filmmakers knew that the mystique of Michael
could never be recaptured - only continued.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:20.030
The sequel is set entirely on the same night
as its predecessor, letting Myers loose in

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:24.610
a hospital for an outing that's much more
gory and violent than the one that came before.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:28.990
But the movie never really justifies its own
existence, setting a standard for all the

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:30.720
unnecessary follow-ups to come.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.980
Halloween H20

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:38.700
Halloween H20 is one of the silliest titles
in horror history, with the series' seventh

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.570
movie having nothing to do with water.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:45.040
Instead, the title refers to the 20 years
that have passed since the first two Halloweens

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:45.600
took place.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.850
It reveals Laurie Strode to be alive again,
living under an assumed name after faking

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:54.960
her death, serving as headmistress of a private
school attended by her teenage son.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:05:01.100
H20 is a Halloween for the Scream generation,
with strong late 90s vibes in its casting

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:01.600
and style.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.880
But while those traits haven't aged that well,
the movie itself has, being a tight, slick

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:10.240
thriller that lets Laurie fight back when
Michael comes for her son.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:12.940
It's Jamie Lee Curtis who makes H20 worth
watching.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.580
Not only is her performance as a haunted,
middle-aged Laurie compelling, the story was

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:22.590
also her idea, with Curtis pitching it as
being about "regaining the soul by facing

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.500
your own fear."

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.620
Hey, isn't that what the new one's about too?

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.900
Halloween III: Season of the Witch

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:32.180
The boldest Halloween sequel tried its hardest
not be a Halloween sequel.

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:37.370
Convinced that more Michael Myers could lead
to diminishing returns, original writers Carpenter

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:41.910
and Hill decided to transform the franchise
into an anthology, with the third film telling

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:43.800
a new story about Halloween night.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.800
The story is about an ancient order of warlocks,
who plan to slaughter children on Halloween

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:51.440
using the popular masks of an evil novelty
company.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:57.410
The masks, powered by microchips containing
pieces of Stonehenge, cause bugs and snakes

00:05:57.410 --> 00:06:01.720
to explode from the heads of their wearers
when they're triggered by a special TV commercial.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:07.460
The attack is vengeance for the commercialization
of Halloween, meant to usher in a new age

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:08.100
of witchcraft.

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:13.900
While that weirdness is refreshing, the movie
itself isn't quite as bonkers as it sounds.

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:17.360
But it's full of memorable moments, and could
have set up a whole new direction for the

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:17.880
series.

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.540
Unfortunately, it was rejected by both viewers
and critics, and the anthology concept was

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:24.160
abandoned.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.460
Halloween (1978)

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:31.120
We can be debate the merits of every Halloween
sequel, but the original is in a class of

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:31.620
its own.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.740
Halloween became legendary by perfectly executing
a simple concept.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.600
Its characters are ordinary and relatable,
and there's no contrived motivations or mythology.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.680
There's just a man in a mask, waiting to kill
you.

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.540
That waiting is the key to Halloween's power.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:51.510
There are surprisingly few murders in the
film, and the killings that do occur are almost

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:52.100
bloodless.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.660
The movie mines horror from dread, not gore,
leaving you breathless once the climax kicks

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:58.160
in.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.220
Add in the shadowy cinematography and Carpenter's
creepy keyboard score, and you've got a minimalist

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:04.970
masterpiece that redefined a genre..

